From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Wed, 28 Oct 2026 04:38:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 39/39] Keep the decoder's dma-buf open until the presenter copies it

Keep the decoder's dma-buf open until the presenter copies it

A compositor handoff hands over a descriptor, not a buffer: export_dmabuf
copies the two plane offsets, the strides and the dma-buf fds the decoder
opened for them, and that POD is all the presenter ever receives. The frame
token that carries it is released as soon as the render pass ends, which drops
the AVFrame that video/ffmpeg.rs retained through drm_frame and lets v4l2
request retire the output surface the frame was decoded into. Weston has not
read the request yet: libwayland queued the add and create_immed pair when the
stream surface sent it, and the compositor imports the planes only when that
message reaches it, so it imports a retired surface and answers "Failed to
import plane 0 as R8" followed by a protocol error that closes the stream
window. This crate's CPU copy hits the same race one step later, as "failed to
map DMA-BUF for the CPU copy: Bad file descriptor (os error 9)".

The producer now keeps a bounded window of the decoder frames whose descriptor
went out to the compositor, oldest first, and the ring is what retires one: a
surface, and the fds its export named, stay alive until the queued CPU copy and
the compositor import have read them. Nothing is duplicated and nothing is
closed here: the export names the decoder's own fds, and the retention is an
extra reference to the frame the producer already owns, so the descriptors the
application counts stay exactly the ones the decoder exported. The two earlier
attempts at this defect are both wrong and neither is kept: owning a duplicate
of the decoder's dma-buf handle replaced DmaBufObject.fd and made the decoder
itself fail, and duplicating a handle per frame made the exported descriptors
differ from the ones the application's buffer cache counts, so v4l2 request
refused packet submission ("Inappropriate ioctl for device") before the EGL
side hit its release-fence assertion.

The route chooser in this file then had a second problem. The interface
describes the import contract it enabled, and an interface that adopts a
device this crate created reports nothing at all: the context log reads
"dmabuf_image_import=false dmabuf_buffer_import=false", and that report alone
sends every DMA-BUF frame through the CPU copy, a three-megabyte memcpy of the
NV12 planes per present that costs a fifth of the rate the decoder produced.
Ask the device instead of the report. The first linear NV12 frame attempts the
VkBuffer import, whose own probe of the device's external-buffer properties is
already in sand::import_linear, and the answer - not the report - decides the
rest of the run: a device that cannot import keeps taking the copy it took
before, and one that can keeps the decoded NV12 on the GPU and lets the render
pass sample the decoder's own planes. Only the buffer bit is derived this way.
The image import contract, which asks the driver for a DMA-BUF image through
the DRM format modifier extensions this one does not offer, stays as the
interface reports it.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [STM32MP25 performance work]
